From: MedPlatform <noreply@medplatform.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] render/egl: an empty device extension string is not fatal

A vendor EGL may advertise EGL_EXT_device_query and return NULL from
eglQueryDeviceStringEXT(EGL_EXTENSIONS). The renderer then refused the
display, and the compositor fell back to drawing in software on a board
whose GPU the display had already been created on.

Nothing read from that string is required: it says whether the device is a
software one and names its DRM node, and on the GBM platform the node comes
from the GBM device. Treat NULL as "no device extensions".

Seen on an STM32MP257F-DK with the vendor's GPU driver, 2026-10-12:
"eglQueryDeviceStringEXT(EGL_EXTENSIONS) failed" at render/egl.c:312.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/render/egl.c	2026-10-12 02:25:16.633317875 -0300
+++ b/render/egl.c	2026-10-12 02:25:16.665319041 -0300
@@ -309,8 +309,14 @@
 		device_exts_str =
 			egl->procs.eglQueryDeviceStringEXT(egl->device, EGL_EXTENSIONS);
 		if (device_exts_str == NULL) {
-			wlr_log(WLR_ERROR, "eglQueryDeviceStringEXT(EGL_EXTENSIONS) failed");
-			return false;
+			/* A driver that offers the query and answers it with nothing.
+			 * Everything asked of the device below is optional - whether
+			 * it is a software one, and its DRM node, which the GBM
+			 * platform already supplies - so it is read as "no device
+			 * extensions" and not as a failure of the display. */
+			wlr_log(WLR_INFO, "eglQueryDeviceStringEXT(EGL_EXTENSIONS) "
+				"returned nothing; continuing without device extensions");
+			device_exts_str = "";
 		}
 
 		if (check_egl_ext(device_exts_str, "EGL_MESA_device_software")) {
